<p>French President Emmanuel Macron publicly criticized President Trump’s shifting statements on the Iran war and NATO during a visit to Seoul on Thursday, saying allies need “stability, calm, a return to peace” rather than a new set of conflicting messages each day. Macron also dismissed the idea of a military operation to “liberate” the Strait of Hormuz, calling it unrealistic. The broader dispute shows how deeply the war with Iran is straining transatlantic unity. Reuters reporting carried by Al-Monitor and The Straits Times said analysts and diplomats see the crisis as one of NATO’s most serious internal strains in years, with President Trump again questioning the alliance’s value. Even amid the sharp rhetoric, European leaders’ repeated calls for peace and alliance stability suggest that diplomacy and collective security remain central goals for key allies.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/uGa5G_6vRkcFLHenb7lro3UV5N_z96l3mW_YYfPOO1sG5Apn6WBKWelFNilx0aFrcnCInbqu7ZIUcCBCUTPraM-dvnVLWyucgyIyWx76I7mrB_GtT_pTczqxXHQDdvkvqJtzrPz0e67dlOPfuV6D6d_1d8UAYIK1JCfh75kDbVg6MDmTzZ5REQWm2-VtGp9hqQAIl1l5V6B9xQ' alt='President Emmanuel Macron of France in Seoul on Thursday. Addressing President Trump’s verbal attacks against NATO, he said, “If you create doubt every day about your commitment, you hollow it out.”' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Asia itinerary: Macron arrived in South Korea after visiting Japan, so the rebuke came during a broader diplomatic swing through Asia rather than at a NATO gathering.</li><li>Confidence as strategy: Macron warned that creating “daily doubt” about commitment can hollow out NATO, stressing that alliance credibility depends on trust as much as military capability.</li><li>Public dissent: The New York Times said Macron is part of a broader group of European officials now openly voicing objections to Trump’s handling of Iran and NATO.</li></ul><blockquote>You have to be serious. When you want to be serious, you don’t say the opposite every day of what you said the day before. And perhaps you shouldn’t talk every day. - Emmanuel Macron</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Emmanuel Macron: He cast the dispute as a matter of seriousness and tone, saying allies need stability and that the Middle East crisis “isn&#x27;t a show.” (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/02/you-have-to-be-serious-macron-criticises-trumps-mixed-messages-about-nato-and-iran'>The Guardian</a>, <a href='https://www.dw.com/en/macron-dismisses-trump-s-inelegant-dig-about-his-marriage/a-76649630'>DW</a>)</li><li>President Trump: Reuters reporting carried by The Straits Times said he was considering withdrawing from NATO, deepening European concern beyond his long-running criticism of the alliance. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/trumps-anger-over-iran-thrusts-nato-into-fresh-crisis'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>Analysts and diplomats: They told Reuters that the striking part of the current crisis is that a war thousands of miles from Europe has still pushed the 76-year-old alliance toward one of its weakest moments. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/analysis-trumps-anger-over-iran-thrusts-nato-fresh-crisis'>Al-Monitor</a>, <a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/trumps-anger-over-iran-thrusts-nato-into-fresh-crisis'>The Straits Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/analysis-trumps-anger-over-iran-thrusts-nato-fresh-crisis'>Analysis-Trump&#x27;s anger over Iran thrusts NATO into fresh crisis</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/02/you-have-to-be-serious-macron-criticises-trumps-mixed-messages-about-nato-and-iran'>‘You have to be serious’: Macron criticises Trump’s mixed messages about Nato and Iran</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1987933/macron-opposes-operation-to-liberate-strait-calls-it-unrealistic'>Macro